<main id="main">
<header class="page-hero container"><span class="section-label">THE PRACTICAL GUIDE — 01</span><h1>Internet speed,<br>without the jargon.</h1><p>Mbps, ping, upload and download are easier to understand than they sound. Here's a practical guide to reading your connection results.</p></header>
<div class="article-shell container"><aside class="article-aside">Speed guide<br>5-minute read</aside><article class="article-body">
<h2>What does Mbps mean?</h2><p>Mbps stands for <strong>megabits per second</strong>. It describes how many bits of data your internet connection can transfer each second. A larger Mbps number generally means more data can move in the same amount of time.</p><p>Mbps is different from MB/s (megabytes per second). One byte contains eight bits, so a 40 Mbps connection has a theoretical transfer rate of around 5 MB/s before overhead and real-world slowdowns.</p>
<h2>Download speed vs upload speed</h2><p><strong>Download speed</strong> is the rate of receiving data from the internet. It matters for streaming, app downloads and loading websites. <strong>Upload speed</strong> is the rate of sending data to the internet. It matters for large attachments, cloud backups, livestreaming and video calls.</p><p>Your internet plan may have much slower upload than download speed. That is normal on some types of broadband.</p>
<h2>What are ping and jitter?</h2><p><strong>Ping</strong> is a measure of round-trip response time, expressed in milliseconds (ms). Lower response times can make games, calls and remote work feel more immediate. <strong>Jitter</strong> describes how much that response time changes between requests. Lower jitter usually means steadier real-time communication.</p><div class="article-callout">This website measures browser request times to its own server. It does not send traditional ICMP ping packets, and it does not measure the latency to every service you use.</div>
<h2>How fast does your internet need to be?</h2><p>The suggestions below are broad starting points for a single activity, not promises. Video quality, household device counts and service requirements vary.</p><table class="guide-table"><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Useful starting point</th><th>What matters most</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Basic browsing & email</td><td>5–10 Mbps</td><td>Stable download</td></tr><tr><td>HD video streaming</td><td>5–10 Mbps per stream</td><td>Download speed</td></tr><tr><td>4K video streaming</td><td>15–25 Mbps per stream</td><td>Consistent download</td></tr><tr><td>Video calls</td><td>3–8 Mbps in both directions</td><td>Upload, ping, jitter</td></tr><tr><td>Online games</td><td>10+ Mbps</td><td>Low ping & jitter</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Why do speed test results change?</h2><p>Even when your plan has not changed, a test may show different results because of Wi-Fi coverage, router load, other people using the connection, your device, network congestion, test server location or the server's own capacity. Mobile connections also change as signal and network demand change.</p><p>For a more useful comparison, run the test a few times, close heavy apps, and repeat at different times of day. If possible, compare Wi-Fi with a wired Ethernet connection.</p>
